---

<FeaturePage
  title="Domain overview"
  metaTitle="Domain overview and domain analysis for any site"
  description="Get a domain overview of any website in your AI agent, with its estimated search traffic, top pages, competitors, backlinks and Domain Rating."
  path="/features/domain-overview"
  lead="Ask your agent for an overview of any domain. It shows the estimated search traffic, the keywords and pages that earn it, the sites it competes with in Google, and how strong its links are. For your own site, it sets those estimates beside your real Search Console clicks."
  {related}
  relatedHeading="Read more"
>
  <h2>What a domain overview shows</h2>
  <p>
    A domain overview is a summary of one website's search footprint. It
    shows how much search traffic the site gets, where that traffic lands,
    who it competes with in Google and how strong its links are. It is the first thing to
    run on a competitor, a site you might buy or a client you are about to
    pitch.
  </p>
  <p>SEO Skill builds the overview from five pieces of research data:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ranked keywords</td><td>How many searches the site ranks for, its estimated monthly search traffic, and which keywords bring the most.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Top pages</td><td>Which pages earn that traffic, and how many keywords each page ranks for in first place, second or third, and fourth to tenth.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Competitors</td><td>The domains that rank for the same keywords, with how many they share.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backlinks</td><td>How many sites link to it, and which ones.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Domain Rating</td><td>How strong its link profile is, from 0 to 100, by <a href="https://ahrefs.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ahrefs</a>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The numbers come from your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key. When the site
    is yours, your agent can also read your Search Console, which is the
    only number in the overview that is not an estimate.
  </p>

  <h2>Tally.so earns about 6,655 estimated US visits a month, 75% on one page</h2>
  <p>
    We ran a domain overview on tally.so, the free online form builder, with
    DataForSEO for the US on 11 November 2026. DataForSEO estimates tally.so
    gets 6,655 visits a month from Google, from 824 ranking keywords and 130
    ranking pages. Its Domain Rating is 92. The four requests cost $0.10 in
    total.
  </p>
  <p>
    The home page earns 4,990 of those 6,655 estimated visits (75%), from 161
    keywords. The login page is second with 713. Every other page, help
    articles and templates included, earns under 160 each.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brand searches carry most of it. Of the 100 keywords that bring tally.so
    the most estimated traffic, the ones that contain "tally" bring 4,144 of
    6,420 visits (65%). Leave out the bare search "tally" and the share is
    56% (2,868 of 5,144), because many of its 40,500 monthly US searches look
    for Tally the accounting software. Tally Solutions shares 198 keywords
    with tally.so, which is how a domain overview shows a brand name
    collision.
  </p>
  <p>
    The biggest search tally.so wins without its name is "online form
    builder". It ranks first for that search, which gets 12,100 US
    searches a month and sends tally.so about 1,362 estimated visits.
  </p>

  <h3>Tally's closest search competitors are Jotform and Typeform</h3>
  <p>
    DataForSEO counted 8,900 domains that share at least one ranking keyword
    with tally.so. These were the 5 form and survey tools with the most
    shared keywords, with each site's Domain Rating by Ahrefs. Google.com
    also shares 430, through Google Forms.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Shared keywords (DR)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>jotform.com</td><td>479 (DR 94)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>typeform.com</td><td>373 (DR 93)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>forms.app</td><td>307 (DR 84)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>surveymonkey.com</td><td>236 (DR 92)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>fillout.com</td><td>227 (DR 89)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Jotform ranks for 203,187 keywords against Tally's 824, so the overlap
    tells you where Tally competes, not that the two are the same size.
  </p>

  <h3>Two template pages sit just off page one for big searches</h3>
  <p>
    The most useful rows in the tally.so overview were not the winners.
    Tally's address form template ranks 14th for "address form" (9,900 US
    searches a month, keyword difficulty 7), and its order form templates
    post ranks 19th for "order form" (4,400 searches, difficulty 6). Both
    searches have low difficulty and a page that already ranks, which is
    where we would start if tally.so were ours.
  </p>

  <h3>53,295 sites link to tally.so</h3>
  <p>
    DataForSEO lists 53,295 referring domains for tally.so. Several of the
    strongest send tens of thousands of links each, such as audiotool.com
    with 172,596 links. A count that large from one site usually means a
    site-wide link or an embedded form rather than one editorial mention
    (a heuristic, so open the site to check). For who links, sorted and
    filtered, the <a href="/tools/backlink-checker">backlink checker</a>{' '}
    goes deeper.
  </p>

  <h2>Run a domain overview in your agent in 3 steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Add the hosted MCP server to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, ChatGPT or any
      client with remote MCP, and sign in with Google. The commands are at
      the top of this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save a <a href="/docs/keys">DataForSEO or Ahrefs key</a> on your
      account page. The provider bills you directly, and every DataForSEO
      result says what the request cost.
    </li>
    <li>Ask for the overview in plain words:</li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock value={`Give me a domain overview of example.com in the US: estimated search traffic, top keywords and pages, top 5 search competitors, backlinks and Domain Rating. Say how much of the traffic comes from brand searches.`} wrap />
  <p>
    The agent runs four research requests and writes the summary. Ask a
    follow-up in the same chat, such as "which pages rank 4th to 20th for
    searches with difficulty under 20?", and it reads the rows it already
    has. A result is kept for a day, so the next page of rows costs nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prefer a terminal? <code>npm i -g seo</code> then <code>seo start</code>{' '}
    gives you the same data through <code>seo research</code>, with the key
    kept on your machine:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research ranked_keywords --target example.com --country us
seo research top_pages --target example.com --country us
seo research competitors --target example.com --country us
seo research backlinks --target example.com`} />
  <p>
    A domain is the whole site with its subdomains. Add a trailing slash to
    read one section, such as <code>example.com/blog/</code>, or give a full
    URL to read one page.
  </p>

  <h2>Set the estimate beside your own Search Console clicks</h2>
  <p>
    For a site you own, the estimate is the weakest number on the screen.
    On 11 November 2026, DataForSEO estimated seoskill.dev at 3 US visits a
    month from 43 keywords. Search Console recorded 47 clicks from the US
    for the 30 days from 10 October to 8 November 2026, and 490 from every
    country. The estimate was about 15 times too low for the US.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gap is normal for a small or new site, because the provider only
    counts keywords in its own database. So read a competitor's estimate
    against another estimate from the same provider, and read your own site
    in Search Console. Your agent keeps the two apart:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`Run a domain overview of example.com, then compare the estimated traffic with my Search Console clicks for the last 28 days by country, and list the pages Search Console shows that the estimate misses.`} wrap />
  <p>
    In a terminal, the <a href="/docs/report">SEO report</a> gives the Search
    Console side for the same site:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo report --site example.com`} wrap />

  <h2>4 things to check in any domain overview</h2>
  <p>
    First, the brand share. A site whose traffic is mostly its own name is
    hard to take traffic from and easy to overrate as an SEO competitor.
    Ask for the share with and without brand keywords, as in the tally.so
    run.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, how many pages carry the traffic. When one page earns most of
    it, the site's search strategy is that page. When the traffic is spread
    across hundreds of guides, the site wins with content, and the top pages
    list tells you which topics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, the keywords in positions 4 to 20 with low difficulty. Those are
    the searches the site is closest to winning, and the same searches are
    open to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fourth, whether the competitor list makes sense. A name collision, a
    single off-topic guide or Google's own products can fill the top of the
    list. The <a href="/features/seo-competitor-analysis">SEO competitor
    analysis</a> page shows how to go from the overview to competitor pages,
    SERPs and link gaps.
  </p>

  <h2>SEO Skill and Semrush Domain Overview compared</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.semrush.com/kb/254-domain-overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Semrush Domain Overview</a>{' '}
    shows a domain's organic and paid traffic, backlinks, Authority Score and
    AI visibility on one screen, with history. A free Semrush account gets 10
    requests a day, and the SEO plan was $139 a month in November 2026,
    according to <a href="https://www.semrush.com/prices" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Semrush's pricing page</a>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>SEO Skill</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What it covers</td><td>Organic search traffic, keywords, top pages, competitors, backlinks and Domain Rating. No paid search data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td>In your agent, or as a command in your terminal.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your own site</td><td>Search Console clicks beside the estimate, kept apart.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it costs</td><td>Your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs usage, about $0.10 an overview with DataForSEO.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    If you need paid search data or a traffic history chart in one place,
    Semrush does that and SEO Skill does not. For a free twelve-month
    traffic trend of any domain, use the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/website-traffic-checker">website traffic checker</a>, and
    to compare the authority of several sites, the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/domain-rating-checker">Domain Rating checker</a>. SEO
    Skill suits you if you want the overview inside the agent that will plan
    and write the pages, next to your own Search Console.
  </p>

  <h2>What a domain overview cannot prove</h2>
  <p>
    Every number in a domain overview of someone else's site is a provider
    estimate. DataForSEO and Ahrefs count keywords and traffic differently,
    and their totals for one site can differ tenfold, so compare sites within
    one provider. An empty list means the provider has nothing for that site
    in its database, not that the site has no traffic or links.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
